- in_text_citation: [(Long et al., 2024-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/be9104873f956f19764c8669b4d23acc948b846d)
- venue: Nature Neuroscience
- citation count: 22
[method]
The paper used simultaneous in vivo fiber photometry of dopamine with a genetically encoded DA sensor and electrophysiological recording of striatal spiking activity in the ventral striatum during a behavioral task. It also used optogenetic manipulation of dopamine signaling to inhibit reward-evoked DA transients and to deliver artificial reward-matched DA transients, then compared striatal spiking activity to baseline or unmanipulated conditions. This relates to the proposed method because it combines optogenetics, genetically encoded neurotransmitter fiber photometry, and electrophysiology to test causal effects of a monoamine signal on striatal activity, but it is limited to DA in the ventral striatum and does not test 5-HT release, DRN SERT neuron stimulation, or slice-level synaptic crosstalk.
[results]
The reported outcome was that inhibiting DA reward transients produced only a small effect on striatal spiking activity, and artificial reward-matched DA transients produced only a small effect on spontaneous striatal activity. In contrast, supra-reward DA signals modulated reward-evoked striatal spiking activity. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical effect sizes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance values, so quantitative outcomes cannot be reported.
[context]
The experimental context was in vivo mouse ventral striatum recordings during a behavioral task, with surgical implantation of a photometry fiber and electrophysiology hardware, expression of a DA sensor in the striatum, and optogenetic manipulation of DA reward transients. The methods included a photometry depth test, a selectivity index, and population decoding. The excerpt does not specify the exact DA sensor, viral constructs, optogenetic stimulation parameters, animal numbers, or statistical thresholds.
[end related work 2]


