The paper used optogenetic laser pulse stimulation of VTA neurons in acute midbrain slices, combined with multi-electrode array recording and spike-train analysis. Coronal 300 µm slices from adult mice were placed on a 3D MEA with 60 electrodes, and a 532 nm laser delivered 10 ms pulses from above the superfused slice. Two pulse-timing protocols were used: six 240 s on/off segments with either fixed 2 Hz pulses or Poisson-distributed pulses at 5 events/s, and six 30 s frequency steps from 0.5 to 5.5 Hz in 0.5 Hz increments, applied in random order and repeated six times. Spikes were detected, sorted, and analyzed using peri-stimulus time histograms, autocorrelation, local variation, phase-response curves normalized to each neuron's intrinsic oscillation frequency, pairwise phase consistency, and partial coherence. Pairwise phase consistency was computed at selected frequencies for the interleaved protocol and at the laser frequencies for the stepped protocol. A surrogate pulse train with identical timing but no laser activation was subtracted to control for timing-related bias in the phase-response curve. This method relates to the proposed plan because both use optogenetic pulse trains to activate VTA dopamine neurons, but the paper tests slice-level spike timing rather than in vivo striatal dopamine or serotonin release, and it does not use the proposed 4 ms, 20 Hz burst protocol.
[results]
Across 73 recorded VTA neurons, laser pulses produced a sharp transient increase in instantaneous firing rate followed by a trough. The averaged phase-response curve showed an approximately 8% phase advance of the next spike, with the largest perturbation near the middle of the intrinsic spike cycle, around phase 0.5, and up to about 10% phase advance. Neurons split by stimulation efficacy around 8 spikes/s, with 43 low-efficacy neurons and 30 high-efficacy neurons, both showed phase-advancing phase-response curves peaking near phase 0.5. The high-efficacy group had a slightly larger phase advance, but the two groups did not differ qualitatively in timing response. Similar results were reported for regular 2 Hz stimulation, and Poisson stimulation was reported to sample stimulus-spike phase relations more effectively.
[context]
Experiments used 7 slices from 7 adult male and female mice aged 6 to 11 weeks, yielding 73 single units. Slices were 300 µm thick, maintained at 32°C in artificial cerebrospinal fluid, and positioned over a 3D MEA with 60 electrodes of 30 µm diameter and 100 µm spacing. The 532 nm laser had 320 mW output, a 100 µm core radius fiber, 0.22 numerical aperture, about 3 mm distance to the MEA, and a stated lower bound of 5 mW/mm2 at recording sites; the intensity estimate was based on light traveling through brain tissue, with additional travel through water. Pulse duration was 10 ms with timing resolution around 20 µs. The laser produced a negative photoelectric artifact on MEA electrodes, which was used to verify that the light spot covered the recording area and made negative-polarity spikes less suitable for analysis. Data were sampled at 20 kHz after 225 Hz high-pass filtering, and pairwise phase consistency was computed with 1 ms bins, Hanning windows, and segments containing a fixed number of cycles. Statistical comparisons across slices used ANOVA, and across-neuron analyses used Spearman rank correlation, t-tests, ANOVA, and jackknife variance estimation for pairwise phase consistency. The paper does not test the proposed 5 episodes of 30 bursts separated by 250 ms, each burst containing 5 pulses of 4 ms at 20 Hz, nor does it measure striatal dopamine or serotonin release, so its relevance is limited to informing that brief VTA laser pulses can drive phase-locked firing and that pulse timing and intensity must be characterized when standardizing optogenetic activation.
[end related work 2]

