[method]
The paper used in vivo fiber photometry of the genetically encoded dopamine sensor dLight in mouse dorsal striatum, specifically DLS, to record real-time dopamine dynamics during systemic pharmacological challenges. In some experiments, dLight photometry was compared with fast-scan cyclic voltammetry, FSCV, to evaluate whether optical and electrochemical dopamine signals changed in the same way. The optical signal was converted to ΔF/F relative to baseline, and transient decay analysis was used to distinguish slow tonic dopamine changes from faster phasic dopamine components. This method is directly relevant to the proposed plan because the proposal also uses fiber photometry with genetically encoded sensors, including dLight1.2, to monitor striatal dopamine dynamics in real time and uses passive cocaine injection as a positive control for sensor response.
[results]
Cocaine produced a robust increase in dLight fluorescence in DLS, supporting its use as a positive control for a dopamine-elevating stimulus in vivo. dLight also reported both slow and fast/phasic dopamine changes during in vivo pharmacological manipulations. In FSCV comparisons, DAT inhibitors increased FSCV signals under standard recording conditions, but the apparent DAT inhibitor effect was altered when FSCV adsorption was changed and was absent in DAT knockout mice. This indicates that apparent DAT inhibitor effects on FSCV can be method-dependent and may not reflect a true increase in dopamine release. The supplied excerpt does not provide exact peak ΔF/F values, effect sizes, or statistical p-values, so the quantitative outcome is limited to the reported presence of a cocaine-evoked dLight signal and the dissociation between dLight and FSCV responses under different FSCV adsorption conditions.
[context]
The experiments were performed in mouse DLS with dLight expressed in dopaminergic terminals and recorded through fiber photometry. Systemic cocaine was used as a dopamine-elevating pharmacological challenge; the exact dose is not specified in the supplied excerpt, but the proposed 15 mg/kg i.p. cocaine dose is a standard positive-control dose for dLight-based dopamine photometry. The paper emphasizes that cocaine is a DAT inhibitor, so it validates sensor response to elevated extracellular dopamine but does not isolate a pure release mechanism. FSCV comparisons were sensitive to electrode adsorption and DAT expression, and DAT knockout mice were used to test whether DAT blockade changes dopamine release. For the proposed DA-5-HT crosstalk plan, this paper supports using cocaine as a positive control for dLight or GRAB5HT fiber photometry in striatal regions, but the control should be interpreted as a dopamine-elevating and reuptake-blocking challenge rather than as a direct measure of optogenetically evoked vesicular release.
