[method]
The paper used in vivo fiber photometry to record phasic dopamine dynamics in mice while they performed an auditory decision-making task. Mice were trained under a controlled water protocol to associate auditory cues with actions and water rewards. After viral expression and fiber implantation, photometric signals were acquired around cue presentation, choice/action, and reward/outcome events. The photometric traces were analyzed as event-locked dopamine signals, compared across learning stages, correct and incorrect choices, and different behavioral strategies, and interpreted using choice models and temporal-difference reinforcement-learning models. This method is related to the proposed research method because both use fiber photometry to capture real-time neurotransmitter dynamics in vivo, although the paper focused on dopamine signals during learned decision-making rather than using passive cocaine injection as a pharmacological positive control.
[results]
The reported outcomes were that cue-locked and outcome-locked dopamine signals evolved differently across learning, that mice learned cue-action-outcome associations, that predictive dopamine signals depended on the behavioral strategy used by the animal, that negative prediction-error signals were present but were not scaled with performance, and that a temporal-difference learning model captured the observed dopamine signatures. The provided excerpt does not include exact effect sizes, p-values, sample sizes, or cocaine-injection comparisons, so no numeric quantitative outcomes can be reported from the supplied text.
[context]
Key experimental conditions included mice, an auditory decision-making task, a controlled water protocol, pilot experiments, viral expression, fiber implantation, histology, fiber photometry, choice modeling, and reinforcement-learning modeling. The excerpt does not specify the exact dopamine sensor, brain region, photometry wavelengths, viral construct, sample size, or statistical thresholds. For the proposed plan, this paper supports the feasibility of fiber photometry for detecting phasic dopamine transients in behaving mice and provides a behavioral and learning context for interpreting cue- and outcome-locked signals, but it does not directly validate the proposed passive cocaine 15 mg/kg positive control or address serotonin measurements.
[end related work 2]

