[method]
The paper used in vivo fiber photometry to record long-term GCaMP6 calcium signals from dopamine and serotonin neurons and analyzed the traces by computing ΔF/F, extracting a baseline from long-term signals, correcting for photobleaching, testing the baseline with a permutation test, and quantifying evoked responses with peak analysis and ROC analysis. This is methodologically related to the proposed MATLAB analysis of fiber photometry because both rely on ΔF/F, baseline normalization, and peak-based comparison of drug-evoked responses, but the paper’s pipeline was applied to neuronal calcium signals after intravenous drug delivery rather than to striatal dLight/GRAB5HT signals after optogenetic stimulation.
[results]
The results sections indicate that cocaine suppressed the activity of both dopamine neurons and serotonin neurons, nicotine activated dopamine neurons and produced biphasic responses, heroin activated both dopamine and serotonin neurons, MDMA profoundly inhibited serotonin neurons, and inhibitory autoreceptors mediated the responses of dopamine and serotonin neurons to cocaine and MDMA. The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical peak amplitudes, AUC values, effect sizes, p-values, or sample sizes, so the magnitude of the cocaine suppression cannot be quantified from the available text.
[context]
The experimental context included AVV vector expression, optical fiber implantation, intravenous catheterization, intravenous drug delivery, and immunohistochemistry, with fiber photometry analysis performed on long-term GCaMP6 signals. The analysis context included baseline extraction, photobleaching correction, permutation testing of baseline, peak analysis, and ROC analysis. Specific animal numbers, recording durations, drug doses, sensor types, stimulation parameters, and statistical thresholds are not provided in the supplied excerpt.
