[method]
The paper’s relevant method is the dopamine recording experiment: in vivo fiber photometry was used to record striatal dopamine while mice performed a visual decision-making task. The fluorescence trace was converted to ΔF/F, normalized to a pre-trial baseline, and summarized over short post-event windows using AUC/peak metrics to compare dopamine transients across trial types. This is directly analogous to the proposed MATLAB analysis of ΔF/F, baseline normalization, 5-second AUC windows, and peak comparisons, except that the paper used behavioral task events rather than optogenetic stimulation or cocaine injection.
[results]
The paper reported that striatal dopamine transients tracked choice-history bias: dopamine was larger on trials with stronger previous-choice or repeat bias and did not simply reflect sensory evidence, current choice, or reward. The supplied excerpt does not include numeric effect sizes, p-values, or sample sizes, so specific quantitative values cannot be reported.
