The experiment was conducted in mice performing a visual decision-making task, with dopamine recorded in the striatum using a genetically encoded dopamine sensor and fiber photometry. The analysis relied on baseline-normalized ΔF/F and AUC/peak summaries of dopamine transients. The paper does not describe optogenetic stimulation or a cocaine positive control; the proposed plan adds VTA/DRN optogenetic stimulation, DA/5-HT sensors, and cocaine injection, making the paper a behavioral dopamine-recording precedent rather than a direct optogenetic/cocaine comparison.
