- venue: Nature Neuroscience
- citation count: 62
[method]
The paper used in vivo fiber photometry with the dopamine sensor dLight1.1 expressed in the striatum to quantify dopamine release after optogenetic activation of cerebellar axons in the substantia nigra pars compacta. Fluorescence traces were converted to ΔF/F relative to a pre-event baseline, and dopamine responses were summarized as area under the curve over 5-second windows around optogenetic stimulation, reward, and baseline periods. This is directly related to the proposed MATLAB-based ΔF/F and 5-second AUC analysis, but the paper applied it to dLight1.1 dopamine signals and cerebellar-to-SNc optogenetic stimulation rather than to cocaine-evoked DA/5-HT responses.
[results]
Optogenetic stimulation of cerebellar axons in the SNc produced dLight1.1 ΔF/F increases in the striatum, indicating that the cerebellar-to-SNc projection drives striatal dopamine release. Cerebellar axon activity in the SNc also carried reward-related information, with activity changes associated with reward prediction, reward delivery, and licking/movement during head-restricted Pavlovian and operant tasks. The supplied excerpt does not provide numeric peak ΔF/F values, AUC magnitudes, sample sizes, or p-values, and it does not report a cocaine-injection comparison.
[context]
The experiments were performed in mice with AAV-mediated expression of dLight1.1 in the striatum and optogenetic actuators in the cerebellum, with fiber optic implantation in the SNc and striatum. Behavioral recordings were collected during head-restricted Pavlovian/operant tasks and random reward tasks with and without licking interruption. The analysis used baseline-normalized ΔF/F and 5-second AUC windows, matching the proposed analysis framework for comparing optogenetic and pharmacological neurotransmitter responses.
[end related work 4]

